<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsongkhapa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1357–1419</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Je Tsongkhapa, Lobsang Drakpa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsongkhapa was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> monk and scholar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amdo">Amdo</a> whose teaching founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school. He stressed monastic discipline and systematic study of philosophy, began the Great Prayer Festival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> in 1409 and in the same year founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-monastery">Ganden Monastery</a>. His disciples founded Drepung and Sera, and the Gelug later became the dominant school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa.jpg" alt="Tsongkhapa" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsonkapa,_16th_century,_Collection_of_Rubin_Museum_of_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
